1
00:00:05,173 --> 00:00:07,051
[GREGSON]
Hey! Watch him! He's running!

2
00:00:07,092 --> 00:00:08,844
[HOLMES]
Quiet, Gregson!

3
00:00:08,886 --> 00:00:13,181
Give me some warning next time.
Did you find another clue?

4
00:00:13,223 --> 00:00:14,600
Perhaps.

5
00:00:15,976 --> 00:00:18,103
[HOLMES]
How curious.

6
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:21,941
[HOLMES] Lestrade, listen.

7
00:00:21,982 --> 00:00:25,444
To solve this...
I need to escape.

8
00:00:25,485 --> 00:00:26,862
[LESTRADE gasps]

9
00:00:28,822 --> 00:00:30,490
[HOLMES]
Hmm.

10
00:02:03,083 --> 00:02:07,046
[GREGSON] The best consulting
detective, indeed!

11
00:02:07,087 --> 00:02:11,383
Hah. We're getting a menace
off the streets for good.

12
00:02:15,095 --> 00:02:19,766
To solve this...
I need to escape.

13
00:02:19,808 --> 00:02:22,352
I can't find the true
culprit behind bars.

14
00:02:22,394 --> 00:02:24,063
When this mystery's concluded,

15
00:02:24,104 --> 00:02:26,773
you and the Yard can
take credit for solving it.

16
00:02:26,815 --> 00:02:30,527
But first, I'm going to need
you to do me this favour.

17
00:02:32,196 --> 00:02:34,114
[LESTRADE]
The true culprit, huh?

18
00:02:34,156 --> 00:02:36,867
You'd better make sure
you do catch them.

19
00:02:36,909 --> 00:02:39,912
Ugh, fine.

20
00:02:39,953 --> 00:02:41,538
[GREGSON yelps]

21
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:42,539
[GREGSON groans]

22
00:02:42,581 --> 00:02:44,249
- -[POLICE 8A] Get him!
- -[POLICE 8B] Oi!

23
00:02:44,290 --> 00:02:46,626
[gasps] Come back here!

24
00:02:46,668 --> 00:02:47,753
Inspector!

25
00:02:47,794 --> 00:02:50,505
Don't blame me!
Something startled the horse!

26
00:03:00,057 --> 00:03:02,017
[HOLMES]
Sorry to keep you waiting.

27
00:03:04,603 --> 00:03:05,938
[WATSON]
Sherlock!

28
00:03:08,314 --> 00:03:11,068
I'm relieved you managed
to slip away from them.

29
00:03:13,612 --> 00:03:15,572
[WATSON]
The inspector took the risk.

30
00:03:15,614 --> 00:03:18,200
Exactly as you
said in your note.

31
00:03:18,242 --> 00:03:19,910
Smart chap. Although,

32
00:03:19,952 --> 00:03:22,996
I'm a wanted man with
Scotland Yard hunting me.

33
00:03:23,038 --> 00:03:24,123
So, John.

34
00:03:24,164 --> 00:03:26,666
Do you still want to get
involved in this case?

35
00:03:26,708 --> 00:03:29,628
Yes, of course.
After all, if you go to prison,

36
00:03:29,669 --> 00:03:30,879
who do you think's
going to end up

37
00:03:30,921 --> 00:03:32,631
having to pay
your half of the rent?

38
00:03:32,672 --> 00:03:34,299
To avoid such a heinous crime,

39
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:35,675
we must settle this quickly.

40
00:03:35,717 --> 00:03:37,136
[WATSON grunts]
Wait.

41
00:03:37,177 --> 00:03:39,805
Do you already know
who the culprit is?

42
00:03:39,846 --> 00:03:42,682
As to their
specific identity, no.

43
00:03:42,724 --> 00:03:44,309
Not yet.

44
00:03:44,350 --> 00:03:47,604
But after laying a trap
with this, we'll lure them in.

45
00:03:47,646 --> 00:03:49,231
Someone's ring.

46
00:03:49,273 --> 00:03:50,523
[WATSON gasps]

47
00:03:50,565 --> 00:03:53,485
[WATSON]
You found it by the desk?

48
00:03:53,526 --> 00:03:56,613
What can I say? I have a rare
talent for pilfering things.

49
00:03:56,655 --> 00:03:59,199
[WATSON]
Mm. Do me a favor.

50
00:03:59,241 --> 00:04:00,533
Going forward,

51
00:04:00,575 --> 00:04:03,328
will you only use these
abilities for the greater good?

52
00:04:04,955 --> 00:04:07,791
[FRED] So, you left
your ring at the scene.

53
00:04:07,833 --> 00:04:09,751
[HOPE]
I'm sorry.

54
00:04:09,793 --> 00:04:13,379
Listen. I need to get it back,
whatever it takes.

55
00:04:13,421 --> 00:04:17,926
It's more precious to me
than my life! [groans]

56
00:04:17,968 --> 00:04:19,845
I'll do my best.

57
00:04:27,519 --> 00:04:29,688
"I found a golden ring

58
00:04:29,729 --> 00:04:34,318
in Three, Lauriston Gardens
off the Brixton Road.

59
00:04:34,359 --> 00:04:38,155
If you might be the owner,
contact Doctor John H. Watson.

60
00:04:38,197 --> 00:04:41,366
Two, two, one, B,
Baker Street?"

61
00:04:41,407 --> 00:04:42,951
Well?

62
00:04:42,993 --> 00:04:45,370
He's setting a trap for us.

63
00:04:47,664 --> 00:04:50,458
Are you sure we
should send Fred then?

64
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:53,419
[WILLIAM] Improvisation
is part of theatre.

65
00:04:53,461 --> 00:04:57,841
And not to mention, there's
Hope's final wish to consider.

66
00:04:57,883 --> 00:04:59,926
No harm in granting it.

67
00:05:01,094 --> 00:05:04,014
[HOLMES]
Good. The carrot is dangled.

68
00:05:04,056 --> 00:05:06,266
Now that's done,
what's our next step?

69
00:05:06,308 --> 00:05:08,143
So glad you asked.

70
00:05:08,185 --> 00:05:10,645
[WIGGINS]
Ready. I've rounded 'em all up.

71
00:05:10,687 --> 00:05:12,438
And you are?

72
00:05:13,439 --> 00:05:16,235
I am the captain of the infamous
Baker Street Irregulars.

73
00:05:16,276 --> 00:05:17,777
Name's Wiggins.

74
00:05:17,819 --> 00:05:21,323
They don't work for you, right?
They're only children.

75
00:05:21,365 --> 00:05:24,910
To the untrained eye,
a band of grimy street urchins.

76
00:05:24,951 --> 00:05:27,371
You didn't have to
call us grimy, guv.

77
00:05:27,412 --> 00:05:28,830
[HOLMES]
They do everything,

78
00:05:28,872 --> 00:05:31,250
from finding lost goods
to running errands.

79
00:05:31,291 --> 00:05:33,626
After all, the whole
city is their home.

80
00:05:33,668 --> 00:05:36,046
They're far more
helpful than the police.

81
00:05:36,088 --> 00:05:37,047
[WIGGINS grunts]

82
00:05:37,089 --> 00:05:39,216
[HOLMES]
Consider them my eyes and ears.

83
00:05:39,258 --> 00:05:40,884
[WIGGINS]
Hm?

84
00:05:40,926 --> 00:05:42,427
We ain't your minions, you know!

85
00:05:42,468 --> 00:05:45,597
And don't expect us to
work for free none either!

86
00:05:45,638 --> 00:05:47,307
Please, you know me.

87
00:05:47,349 --> 00:05:50,185
You will receive your reward,
after you finish the job.

88
00:05:50,227 --> 00:05:54,314
I'm confused. What exactly
are these lads going to do?

89
00:05:54,356 --> 00:05:56,775
Just provide a
little assistance.

90
00:06:02,906 --> 00:06:05,200
[WATSON]
Lucy, hmm?

91
00:06:05,242 --> 00:06:07,119
So it's a women's wedding band.

92
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:11,581
And judging by the design,
not an aristocratic union.

93
00:06:11,623 --> 00:06:13,208
By Sherlock's deduction,

94
00:06:13,250 --> 00:06:15,794
the culprit dropped this
at the crime scene.

95
00:06:15,835 --> 00:06:18,297
Basically, it's
our only evidence.

96
00:06:18,338 --> 00:06:20,506
If it belongs to the killer,

97
00:06:20,548 --> 00:06:23,927
they're certain to
come looking for it.

98
00:06:23,969 --> 00:06:26,763
[HOLMES] It would be
the most logical answer.

99
00:06:26,805 --> 00:06:31,476
The real killer was much
larger in stature than Drebber.

100
00:06:31,517 --> 00:06:35,855
[WATSON]
In person he must be a giant.

101
00:06:35,897 --> 00:06:36,982
[WATSON grunts]

102
00:06:40,319 --> 00:06:41,111
[knocking]

103
00:06:41,153 --> 00:06:42,446
[WATSON gasps]

104
00:06:43,822 --> 00:06:47,075
[WATSON]
Yes? How may I... help... you?

105
00:06:47,117 --> 00:06:49,202
[OLD WOMAN] I'm here
regarding the missing ring

106
00:06:49,244 --> 00:06:51,997
you posted about
in the newspaper.

107
00:06:52,038 --> 00:06:53,706
Thank you for the advert.

108
00:06:53,748 --> 00:06:56,876
Otherwise, my Lucy might
have lost it for good.

109
00:06:56,918 --> 00:06:58,337
[WATSON]
An old woman?

110
00:06:58,378 --> 00:06:59,879
[OLD WOMAN]
My daughter's name should be

111
00:06:59,921 --> 00:07:02,674
inscribed on the inside
of the ring, yes?

112
00:07:02,715 --> 00:07:04,009
[WATSON]
Uh, it is.

113
00:07:04,050 --> 00:07:05,718
[OLD WOMAN]
She believes she dropped it

114
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:07,637
somewhere near Number Three.

115
00:07:07,679 --> 00:07:11,683
She only just got married
last year. It's tragic.

116
00:07:11,724 --> 00:07:14,978
If her husband finds out
she lost her precious ring,

117
00:07:15,020 --> 00:07:18,482
he would be terribly
angry with her.

118
00:07:18,523 --> 00:07:19,941
[WATSON grunts]

119
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:25,071
Well, we can't have that.
Here you are.

120
00:07:57,854 --> 00:08:00,399
[gasps]

121
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:03,193
Spry for a little old lady!

122
00:08:08,323 --> 00:08:10,033
[HOLMES]
Hmm.

123
00:08:25,965 --> 00:08:32,305
- -[OLD WOMAN gasps]
- -[HOLMES grunts]

124
00:08:32,347 --> 00:08:34,057
[HOLMES yells]

125
00:08:35,058 --> 00:08:38,311
[FRED groans]

126
00:08:38,353 --> 00:08:40,730
Now, to find out
who you really are--

127
00:08:40,772 --> 00:08:42,982
[HOLMES gasps, shouts]

128
00:08:44,067 --> 00:08:45,860
You there, stop!

129
00:08:57,372 --> 00:08:59,207
Damn! I should've
known they wouldn't

130
00:08:59,249 --> 00:09:01,460
let themselves get
caught so easily.

131
00:09:01,501 --> 00:09:03,587
They got one over on me.

132
00:09:05,797 --> 00:09:07,591
[WATSON]
That man just now,

133
00:09:07,632 --> 00:09:10,676
was he the mastermind
we're searching for?

134
00:09:10,718 --> 00:09:13,346
[HOLMES] No. Probably just
one of his associates.

135
00:09:13,388 --> 00:09:16,558
[WATSON] Meaning there
are more. In that case,

136
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:19,561
you believe they're some
sort of organization?

137
00:09:19,603 --> 00:09:21,687
More than likely, yes.

138
00:09:21,729 --> 00:09:23,523
But that'll have
to wait for the moment

139
00:09:23,565 --> 00:09:26,192
as finding Drebber's
killer comes first.

140
00:09:26,234 --> 00:09:27,486
But how?

141
00:09:27,527 --> 00:09:29,946
That ring was the only lead
we had and now it's gone.

142
00:09:29,988 --> 00:09:32,907
What? No need to be so gloomy.

143
00:09:32,949 --> 00:09:35,994
At this point, John,
the crime is all but solved.

144
00:09:36,035 --> 00:09:37,287
[gasps]

145
00:09:37,329 --> 00:09:40,373
Are you telling me you know
who murdered Lord Drebber?

146
00:09:40,415 --> 00:09:41,333
Sure.

147
00:09:41,374 --> 00:09:43,835
Well then, who on
earth is the killer?

148
00:09:44,919 --> 00:09:47,297
[HOLMES]
Perfect.

149
00:09:47,339 --> 00:09:50,383
Why don't you wait
for me in there?

150
00:09:50,425 --> 00:09:52,344
I shan't be long.

151
00:09:52,385 --> 00:09:54,304
But, Sherlock.

152
00:09:54,346 --> 00:09:56,306
Wait! Where're you...?

153
00:10:06,774 --> 00:10:08,610
[WATSON]
Unbelievable.

154
00:10:08,652 --> 00:10:11,988
Did he really discover
who the killer is?

155
00:10:12,030 --> 00:10:14,907
Why'd he have to
keep me in suspense?

156
00:10:14,949 --> 00:10:16,951
[WATSON]
Hm?

157
00:10:16,993 --> 00:10:19,663
All done. Let's go.

158
00:10:22,582 --> 00:10:25,126
[HOLMES]
This arrived from Lestrade.

159
00:10:25,168 --> 00:10:26,545
Take a look.

160
00:10:28,547 --> 00:10:30,674
[HOLMES]
Lord Drebber's past.

161
00:10:30,715 --> 00:10:33,677
I asked the inspector
to check into it for me.

162
00:10:33,718 --> 00:10:36,429
The man was a
philanderer without equal.

163
00:10:36,471 --> 00:10:37,805
With a particular fondness for

164
00:10:37,847 --> 00:10:40,642
molesting women
of lower standing.

165
00:10:40,684 --> 00:10:43,811
We can't begin to imagine
what they were forced to endure.

166
00:10:43,853 --> 00:10:45,188
If a victim threatened to talk,

167
00:10:45,230 --> 00:10:48,525
he usually settled
the matter with money.

168
00:10:48,567 --> 00:10:49,859
Unfortunately,

169
00:10:49,901 --> 00:10:53,279
there was one who proved to be
an exception to this rule.

170
00:10:53,321 --> 00:10:55,031
Lucy Ferrier.

171
00:10:55,073 --> 00:10:57,450
She had recently
married a local farmer

172
00:10:57,492 --> 00:11:00,286
by the name of Jefferson Hope.

173
00:11:00,328 --> 00:11:04,832
When her body was discovered,
her remains were returned home.

174
00:11:04,874 --> 00:11:07,502
Sadly, there were
a significant number

175
00:11:07,544 --> 00:11:10,505
of horrifying
bruises to the body.

176
00:11:13,007 --> 00:11:17,554
[WATSON grunts] This is a
positively tragic story.

177
00:11:17,596 --> 00:11:20,056
You're saying,
the true killer is--

178
00:11:20,098 --> 00:11:22,767
[HOLMES] The man who
took Lord Drebber's life,

179
00:11:22,808 --> 00:11:26,605
is the same Jefferson
Hope, murderer...

180
00:11:26,646 --> 00:11:29,941
...and our driver, oddly enough.

181
00:11:29,982 --> 00:11:31,192
[gasps]

182
00:11:31,234 --> 00:11:35,405
Isn't that right,
Jefferson Hope?

183
00:11:37,115 --> 00:11:41,703
I must say, I'm surprised you
investigated so thoroughly.

184
00:11:41,745 --> 00:11:43,622
You know, having
your carriage driven

185
00:11:43,663 --> 00:11:46,583
by a murderer isn't something
many people get to experience.

186
00:11:46,625 --> 00:11:48,585
Look, I'm not doubting
your methods, Holmes,

187
00:11:48,627 --> 00:11:50,378
but even if the
inscription on the ring

188
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:52,046
is connected to
this Jefferson Hope,

189
00:11:52,088 --> 00:11:54,173
that's still not
enough evidence to prove

190
00:11:54,215 --> 00:11:56,718
that he's the same man
as our cab driver!

191
00:11:56,760 --> 00:12:00,179
You raise a good point.
I arrived at my conclusion from

192
00:12:00,221 --> 00:12:02,348
two different lines
of reasoning.

193
00:12:02,390 --> 00:12:06,185
The first one was
the ring that we found.

194
00:12:06,227 --> 00:12:09,397
The second was a set
of wheel tracks.

195
00:12:09,439 --> 00:12:11,857
Thanks to some rain earlier
that evening at the scene,

196
00:12:11,899 --> 00:12:14,110
they were quite visible.

197
00:12:14,152 --> 00:12:16,613
All the houses
belonged to noblemen,

198
00:12:16,655 --> 00:12:20,866
who own four-wheeled carriages,
driven by two-horses.

199
00:12:20,908 --> 00:12:23,787
However, in front of
the Earl's manor,

200
00:12:23,828 --> 00:12:27,165
there were smaller tracks
belonging to a narrower cab.

201
00:12:28,667 --> 00:12:30,001
No killer would
be foolish enough

202
00:12:30,042 --> 00:12:32,044
to arrive in a carriage,
commit the murder,

203
00:12:32,086 --> 00:12:34,589
hop back in the cab,
then return home.

204
00:12:34,631 --> 00:12:36,048
Otherwise,
the driver would have had

205
00:12:36,090 --> 00:12:39,177
a clear view of
whoever the killer was.

206
00:12:39,218 --> 00:12:40,637
Which means...

207
00:12:40,679 --> 00:12:44,683
...the culprit was someone who
bore no risk of that happening.

208
00:12:44,724 --> 00:12:46,601
What remains?

209
00:12:46,643 --> 00:12:50,146
He would have had to
drive himself there.

210
00:12:50,188 --> 00:12:52,398
In response,
I sent the Irregulars off

211
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:55,694
in search of someone who
met the following criteria:

212
00:12:55,735 --> 00:12:57,445
A driver of a hansom cab,

213
00:12:57,487 --> 00:12:59,989
who was a large man
with pudgy fingers.

214
00:13:00,031 --> 00:13:02,158
Additionally, they would have
gone to Number Three

215
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:03,785
the day of the crime.

216
00:13:03,827 --> 00:13:06,037
One person came up right away.

217
00:13:06,078 --> 00:13:08,748
[WATSON]
Let me guess, Jefferson Hope.

218
00:13:09,833 --> 00:13:12,502
Despite not knowing the
contents of Lestrade's report,

219
00:13:12,544 --> 00:13:15,839
the Irregulars
turned up the same man.

220
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:17,006
[HOLMES]
In short,

221
00:13:17,048 --> 00:13:20,176
It wasn't difficult to
put a name to the shooter.

222
00:13:20,218 --> 00:13:22,178
Case closed.

223
00:13:22,220 --> 00:13:23,555
One question.

224
00:13:23,596 --> 00:13:25,931
The manner in which
you killed Drebber--

225
00:13:25,973 --> 00:13:27,726
why so coarse?

226
00:13:27,767 --> 00:13:30,311
The plan demanded
precision on all levels.

227
00:13:30,353 --> 00:13:33,939
Elegance, yet your
execution was sloppy.

228
00:13:35,859 --> 00:13:37,151
[HOPE]
It's true.

229
00:13:37,193 --> 00:13:39,945
Though his death was to be
carried out with more finesse,

230
00:13:39,987 --> 00:13:42,824
I couldn't help myself!

231
00:13:42,866 --> 00:13:46,285
Not when I came
face to face with Drebber.

232
00:13:55,587 --> 00:13:58,089
You expected me to
remain level-headed?

233
00:13:58,130 --> 00:14:00,007
Seeing that monster?

234
00:14:00,049 --> 00:14:03,177
The man who killed my wife?

235
00:14:03,219 --> 00:14:06,806
[HOPE]
Well, Sherlock Holmes...

236
00:14:06,848 --> 00:14:09,851
...how about making
a deal with me?

237
00:14:24,949 --> 00:14:26,200
Be smart, Mister Hope.

238
00:14:26,242 --> 00:14:28,327
You've already achieved
your goal, haven't you?

239
00:14:28,369 --> 00:14:31,997
Is that not enough?
Turn yourself into the police.

240
00:14:32,039 --> 00:14:33,666
Turn myself in?

241
00:14:33,708 --> 00:14:34,751
[groans]

242
00:14:34,793 --> 00:14:37,503
[HOPE coughs]

243
00:14:40,924 --> 00:14:45,052
A futile gesture. You see,
I don't have much time left.

244
00:14:45,094 --> 00:14:48,264
At this stage of the game,
I may as well be dead.

245
00:14:48,306 --> 00:14:52,310
All's lost. I gave all
I had to my benefactor.

246
00:14:52,351 --> 00:14:55,271
This deal--and what,
pray tell, are the terms?

247
00:14:55,313 --> 00:14:57,857
It's a simple matter, gentlemen.

248
00:14:59,150 --> 00:15:00,526
[WATSON gasps]

249
00:15:02,862 --> 00:15:04,405
[HOPE]
My life has been destroyed

250
00:15:04,447 --> 00:15:07,241
by the corruption
of aristocrats.

251
00:15:07,283 --> 00:15:12,664
My tale's at an end, and
I want you to close the book.

252
00:15:12,705 --> 00:15:17,168
In other words, pick up the
gun and end this suffering.

253
00:15:17,209 --> 00:15:18,294
[grunts]

254
00:15:18,336 --> 00:15:21,422
[HOPE] Since you uncovered
Drebber's vile sins,

255
00:15:21,464 --> 00:15:25,134
it's only fitting
that I die by your hand.

256
00:15:27,136 --> 00:15:28,262
[HOLMES]
Hm?

257
00:15:29,639 --> 00:15:31,182
[HOLMES]
And who's that?

258
00:15:31,223 --> 00:15:34,727
[HOPE] The one who got
Lucy's ring back for me.

259
00:15:38,063 --> 00:15:40,024
[HOPE] Events have
been carefully arranged

260
00:15:40,065 --> 00:15:41,526
after I'm dead.

261
00:15:41,567 --> 00:15:46,280
Kill me and he'll reveal the
identity of my benefactor.

262
00:15:46,322 --> 00:15:47,532
[HOLMES]
Tending to your affairs before

263
00:15:47,573 --> 00:15:51,661
you're even gone from this
world. How very thorough.

264
00:15:51,703 --> 00:15:53,287
Yes, indeed.

265
00:15:53,329 --> 00:15:56,290
He truly is an incredible
person.

266
00:15:57,333 --> 00:15:59,293
[HOPE] When I arrived
at Drebber's mansion

267
00:15:59,335 --> 00:16:02,839
at the appointed time,
the door opened as if by magic,

268
00:16:02,881 --> 00:16:05,717
and there was nary a soul
around to interfere.

269
00:16:05,758 --> 00:16:09,303
The man is a true
criminal genius.

270
00:16:09,345 --> 00:16:12,473
My benefactor holds
you in high regard.

271
00:16:12,515 --> 00:16:14,141
Enough to want your name

272
00:16:14,183 --> 00:16:18,145
left at the scene to see
if you could solve the case.

273
00:16:18,187 --> 00:16:20,147
But to what end, though?

274
00:16:20,189 --> 00:16:23,067
Why engineer such
an elaborate scheme?

275
00:16:23,108 --> 00:16:25,444
[HOPE]
You wanna know?

276
00:16:25,486 --> 00:16:30,783
Pull the trigger and
all will be revealed.

277
00:16:30,825 --> 00:16:32,535
Tempting,

278
00:16:32,577 --> 00:16:36,706
but seeing how I'm considered
a murder suspect on the run,

279
00:16:36,748 --> 00:16:37,790
I don't think killing you

280
00:16:37,832 --> 00:16:40,752
would do much in the
way of clearing my name.

281
00:16:40,793 --> 00:16:42,545
Don't worry about that.

282
00:16:42,587 --> 00:16:46,090
Once you shoot me, he'll arrange
for a witness to come forward,

283
00:16:46,131 --> 00:16:50,720
providing testimony that'll all
but guarantee your freedom.

284
00:16:50,762 --> 00:16:54,682
You see? The arrangement
benefits you as well.

285
00:16:54,724 --> 00:16:58,770
Kill me and both of us
will get exactly what we desire.

286
00:16:59,729 --> 00:17:02,273
Make your decision, sir.

287
00:17:12,283 --> 00:17:13,826
[HOLMES]
You're right.

288
00:17:13,868 --> 00:17:16,746
It would prove a great
relief to the both of us.

289
00:17:19,498 --> 00:17:21,084
I can't let you do that!

290
00:17:21,125 --> 00:17:23,086
You know that this is wrong!

291
00:17:24,629 --> 00:17:26,005
[HOLMES]
How so?

292
00:17:26,047 --> 00:17:27,423
He gets a quick death

293
00:17:27,465 --> 00:17:29,884
and I get the identity
of the mastermind.

294
00:17:29,926 --> 00:17:31,260
That may be true.

295
00:17:31,302 --> 00:17:34,221
But shoot that gun,
and there's no going back!

296
00:17:34,263 --> 00:17:36,557
[HOPE]
Don't listen to him!

297
00:17:36,599 --> 00:17:39,226
You're a man who
seeks out mysteries.

298
00:17:39,268 --> 00:17:42,271
There's no satisfaction
without a solution.

299
00:17:42,313 --> 00:17:43,689
And you want to know who

300
00:17:43,731 --> 00:17:46,484
created the ultimate
mystery, don't you?

301
00:17:46,525 --> 00:17:50,362
So, shoot me and
you'll have your answer!

302
00:17:50,404 --> 00:17:52,031
[HOLMES grunts]

303
00:17:55,200 --> 00:17:58,704
No, Sherlock, please!
Put it down!

304
00:17:58,746 --> 00:18:00,748
Don't do it!

305
00:18:01,124 --> 00:18:03,208
[gun fires]

306
00:18:08,089 --> 00:18:11,092
[HOPE grunts]

307
00:18:12,844 --> 00:18:15,138
[gasps]

308
00:18:15,179 --> 00:18:19,183
[HOLMES laughing]

309
00:18:19,224 --> 00:18:21,352
So sorry. I won't kill you.

310
00:18:21,393 --> 00:18:22,979
[HOPE] You're a fool if you
don't!

311
00:18:23,021 --> 00:18:25,690
Their identity will stay a
secret!

312
00:18:25,731 --> 00:18:27,399
[HOLMES] I'll have to figure it
out on my own?

313
00:18:27,441 --> 00:18:29,234
Huh. Now imagine that.

314
00:18:29,276 --> 00:18:32,571
Please, enough insults,
you daft bastard.

315
00:18:32,613 --> 00:18:33,865
See here!

316
00:18:33,906 --> 00:18:36,617
The satisfaction is finding
the solution on your own!

317
00:18:36,659 --> 00:18:37,660
There's always some sod

318
00:18:37,702 --> 00:18:39,996
that comes along and
tries spoiling my fun!

319
00:18:40,038 --> 00:18:41,914
I'll have none of it!

320
00:18:43,332 --> 00:18:45,376
[WATSON]
Oh, thank God.

321
00:18:46,919 --> 00:18:49,881
Seriously, thank God.

322
00:18:49,922 --> 00:18:52,591
- -[HOLMES] John?
- -[WATSON] I'm so sorry!

323
00:18:52,633 --> 00:18:54,343
[HOLMES]
Just pull yourself together!

324
00:18:54,385 --> 00:18:56,220
[WATSON] Please forgive me
for doubting you!

325
00:18:56,261 --> 00:18:57,346
Even for a moment!

326
00:18:57,388 --> 00:19:00,641
[HOLMES] You're forgiven.
Just get off of me!

327
00:19:02,351 --> 00:19:03,435
[HOLMES]
Truly,

328
00:19:03,477 --> 00:19:06,271
I'm sorry I wasn't able
to grant your request.

329
00:19:06,313 --> 00:19:07,815
Your final wish.

330
00:19:07,857 --> 00:19:09,400
[HOPE gasps]

331
00:19:17,491 --> 00:19:19,952
[inhales]

332
00:19:19,994 --> 00:19:21,620
Ah, Scotland Yard!

333
00:19:21,662 --> 00:19:24,082
Have you bungled
any cases, lately?

334
00:19:24,123 --> 00:19:26,333
I'd like you to meet someone.

335
00:19:26,375 --> 00:19:30,379
Allow me to present the actual
killer of Lord Enoch J. Drebber,

336
00:19:30,421 --> 00:19:32,048
Jefferson Hope!

337
00:19:32,090 --> 00:19:34,425
Sherlock Holmes?! Why is...?!

338
00:19:35,885 --> 00:19:37,386
You caught him!

339
00:19:39,138 --> 00:19:42,266
I kept my end of
the bargain as promised.

340
00:19:45,686 --> 00:19:49,356
[WILLIAM] Consulting
detective, Sherlock Holmes.

341
00:19:50,942 --> 00:19:52,902
[WILLIAM]
Somehow, he managed to slip away

342
00:19:52,944 --> 00:19:55,529
from Scotland Yard
and solve the case.

343
00:19:55,571 --> 00:19:56,697
Thoughts?

344
00:19:56,739 --> 00:19:59,117
Very impressive the way
he discerned the murder

345
00:19:59,158 --> 00:20:01,535
was planned by
a separate individual.

346
00:20:01,577 --> 00:20:03,704
I would say his
competency as a detective

347
00:20:03,746 --> 00:20:05,831
appears to be quite genuine.

348
00:20:05,873 --> 00:20:07,208
The audition provided us

349
00:20:07,250 --> 00:20:09,877
with all the answers
we had hoped it would.

350
00:20:09,919 --> 00:20:11,837
We knew what sort
of mind he had,

351
00:20:11,879 --> 00:20:14,506
but nothing of his heart.

352
00:20:14,548 --> 00:20:15,841
My goal

353
00:20:15,883 --> 00:20:17,927
was to see whether
he'd resort to any means

354
00:20:17,969 --> 00:20:21,847
in order to achieve his,
and he won't.

355
00:20:21,889 --> 00:20:22,932
Even after we offered him

356
00:20:22,974 --> 00:20:25,810
all the answers on
a silver platter, no dice.

357
00:20:25,851 --> 00:20:28,187
He still didn't go
for Hope's proposal.

358
00:20:28,229 --> 00:20:30,397
[LOUIS]
According to William's analysis,

359
00:20:30,439 --> 00:20:33,358
Holmes has a keen interest
in finding the mastermind.

360
00:20:33,400 --> 00:20:36,320
The one pulling the
strings from the dark.

361
00:20:36,361 --> 00:20:39,657
And yet he refused to kill Hope.

362
00:20:39,698 --> 00:20:41,284
When it comes to
accomplishing his goals,

363
00:20:41,325 --> 00:20:44,245
he has limits, making
him different from us.

364
00:20:44,287 --> 00:20:46,205
Then he's worthy, right,

365
00:20:46,247 --> 00:20:48,666
this man, Holmes?

366
00:20:49,708 --> 00:20:51,627
From here on out,

367
00:20:51,669 --> 00:20:54,005
there will be a new character
in this crime drama

368
00:20:54,046 --> 00:20:55,173
we've been staging.

369
00:20:55,214 --> 00:20:58,259
A detective striving
to solve the case.

370
00:21:00,011 --> 00:21:04,765
[WILLIAM] I believe
we have found our leading man.

371
00:21:04,807 --> 00:21:08,435
A beacon in a dark world.

372
00:21:12,857 --> 00:21:14,984
[WILLIAM]
Sherlock Holmes.

373
00:21:26,871 --> 00:21:29,165
[WATSON]
Is it morning already?

374
00:21:32,376 --> 00:21:35,379
I think I'll go downstairs
and make some tea.

375
00:21:37,131 --> 00:21:41,010
[HOLMES snoring]

376
00:21:44,263 --> 00:21:47,058
[WATSON] As long as
I'm living here with you,

377
00:21:47,099 --> 00:21:50,811
I'll never be in want
of writing material.

378
00:21:50,853 --> 00:21:52,855
I'm sure of it.

379
00:23:33,372 --> 00:23:36,459
[WILLIAM] Next time
on Moriarty the Patriot  .

380
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:39,211
"The Two Detectives. Act One."
